  "summary": "Joint chromatin and transcriptome profiling at single-cell resolution now extends to human and primate basal ganglia, with population-level variation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is single-cell multiomic profiling of basal ganglia and striatal tissue, linking chromatin state, transcriptional identity and cell-type organization. A human atlas profiles histone modifications and transcriptomes across eight basal ganglia regions with a deep-learning model. A cross-species study of 1.8M primate nuclei defines 61 conserved cell types and maps disease risk to populations. Spatial transcriptomics extends the comparison to human, macaque and marmoset basal ganglia. A striatal study of 3.9M nuclei from 178 donors quantifies cell-abundance and expression variation with age and common eQTLs. Broader adult brain atlases of human and mouse extend regulatory mapping beyond basal ganglia. What is new is joint measurement of regulatory layers at single-cell resolution, combined with cross-species comparison and population-level variation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-14"
}
